ARC = dict(id="oceania", short="Oceania", title="Oceania: Voyagers, Convicts and New Nations", span=[1606,2023], region="oc",
 summary="Polynesian, Melanesian and Micronesian peoples had settled the Pacific's islands, and Aboriginal Australians the continent, long before Europeans arrived. "
  "Dutch ships charted Australia's coasts in the 1600s, but it was James Cook's voyages and American independence, which closed America to British convicts, that led to the First Fleet in 1788. "
  "British colonisation brought frontier wars, epidemics and dispossession; in New Zealand, the Treaty of Waitangi of 1840 was followed by wars over land. Gold rushes, federation and the ANZAC landing at Gallipoli shaped new national identities, often defined by the White Australia policy. "
  "The Pacific War brought fighting to New Guinea and the Solomons, and nuclear testing followed. Since the 1960s, Pacific island nations have won independence, and Australia and New Zealand have begun to reckon with their colonial past.",
 chapters=[("First contacts",1606,1787,"Dutch and British navigators reach Australia and New Zealand."),
           ("Colonies and frontiers",1788,1900,"Convicts, gold, treaties and wars over land."),
           ("New nations in a world at war",1901,1945,"Federation, Gallipoli and the Pacific War."),
           ("A new Pacific",1946,2023,"Nuclear tests, independence and reckoning.")],
 reading=["Stuart Macintyre, A Concise History of Australia (5th ed., 2020)","James Belich, Making Peoples: A History of the New Zealanders (1996)",
          "Nicholas Thomas, Islanders: The Pacific in the Age of Empire (2010)","Henry Reynolds, The Other Side of the Frontier (1981)"])

